WEBVTT

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:02.625
One coefficient has many contours.

00:00:02.625 --> 00:00:04.518
Pick one Legendre degree, n.

00:00:04.518 --> 00:00:10.144
The coefficient measures how much of that polynomial occurs in the function on the red interval.

00:00:10.144 --> 00:00:13.999
The surrounding ellipse gives us another way to measure the same number.

00:00:13.999 --> 00:00:17.406
Watch the contour move out while the coefficient stays put.

00:00:17.406 --> 00:00:19.895
The estimate above it has three ingredients.

00:00:19.895 --> 00:00:28.870
The largest allowed function value sets a scale, the perimeter measures how far we integrate, and the kernel supplies a power that shrinks with degree.

00:00:28.870 --> 00:00:33.237
Lesson four, Bandtlow’s Lemma one point seven, gives us the contour.

00:00:33.237 --> 00:00:37.839
Lesson five, Lemma one point eight, gives us the decaying kernel waves.

00:00:37.839 --> 00:00:42.355
Together they prove the coefficient estimate in Lemma one point nine.

00:00:42.355 --> 00:00:44.166
Start on the real interval.

00:00:44.166 --> 00:00:48.021
Take the function one divided by two and a half minus x.

00:00:48.021 --> 00:00:49.529
Choose degree two.

00:00:49.529 --> 00:00:54.002
Its Legendre polynomial is one half of three x squared minus one.

00:00:54.002 --> 00:00:58.817
Multiply these two functions and sweep the signed area from minus one to one.

00:00:58.817 --> 00:01:02.737
The central region subtracts area and the two ends add it back.

00:01:02.737 --> 00:01:09.578
Multiplying the result by five halves gives a coefficient of about zero point zero four nine four two.

00:01:09.578 --> 00:01:12.943
Its definition uses only real inputs.

00:01:12.943 --> 00:01:15.159
A circle draws each inner ellipse.

00:01:15.159 --> 00:01:18.594
Choose the outer circle of radius rho in the z plane.

00:01:18.594 --> 00:01:27.981
Its image under the Joukowski map is the boundary of the outer ellipse, where our example is bounded, and the theorem assumes boundedness inside.

00:01:27.981 --> 00:01:32.611
Choose the smaller circle of radius s, with s between one and rho.

00:01:32.611 --> 00:01:36.175
The cyan spoke has length s and turns with the point z.

00:01:36.175 --> 00:01:41.850
Its image, the gold point omega, runs along the boundary of the inner ellipse, E sub s.

00:01:41.850 --> 00:01:44.808
At angle zero, omega is at the right tip.

00:01:44.808 --> 00:01:47.447
At a quarter turn, omega is at the top.

00:01:47.447 --> 00:01:54.935
The subscript s records which circle produced the ellipse, while its horizontal and vertical semiaxes have different lengths.

00:01:54.935 --> 00:01:59.431
The entire closed inner ellipse lies inside the open outer one.

00:01:59.431 --> 00:02:02.137
The nearest point gives the largest value.

00:02:02.137 --> 00:02:05.331
For our example, the cross at two and a half is the pole.

00:02:05.331 --> 00:02:10.445
The size of the function is one over the distance from the moving point to that cross.

00:02:10.445 --> 00:02:14.435
Trace the outer ellipse until the moving point reaches its right tip.

00:02:14.435 --> 00:02:18.638
The mint segment goes from the centre to this tip along the real axis.

00:02:18.638 --> 00:02:22.052
Its length, a sub rho, is the horizontal semiaxis.

00:02:22.052 --> 00:02:29.178
To find it, apply Joukowski at angle zero, where z equals rho and its reciprocal equals one over rho.

00:02:29.178 --> 00:02:34.171
The gold distance from the tip to the pole is therefore two and a half minus a sub rho.

00:02:34.171 --> 00:02:39.590
Interior points approach that tip, so its reciprocal is the supremum in the open ellipse.

00:02:39.590 --> 00:02:45.081
At outer radius two point one five, this gives about zero point eight three nine.

